        <h1 class='static-page__title--sinai'>Sinai Palimpsests Project</h1>
      </div>

      <div class='static-page__row'>
        <p class='static-page__text static-page__text--sinai'>
          The Sinai Palimpsests Project was a collaboration between St. Catherine's Monastery of the Sinai, the Early Manuscripts Electronic Library, and the UCLA Library, generously funded by the Arcadia Fund over a six-year period. The project used multispectral imaging to recover erased texts within 74 of the more than 180 palimpsests now known to be held in the Monastery's library — manuscripts whose parchment had been scraped clean and reused for new writing by later scribes, leaving the erased, older text faintly recoverable beneath the surface. Prior to the project, only three of Sinai's known palimpsests had been thoroughly studied and published in scholarly editions.
        </p>
        <p class='static-page__text static-page__text--sinai'>
          Working from the resulting spectral images, an international team of scholars identified more than 300 different erased texts in eleven languages — Greek, Syriac, Georgian, Arabic, Christian Palestinian Aramaic, Latin, Caucasian Albanian, Armenian, Slavonic, Coptic, and Ethiopic — many surviving nowhere else. Their findings substantially expanded the body of texts available to historians, linguists, and palaeographers, and provided new evidence for reconstructing the history of the Monastery and of the communities who passed through it.
        </p>
        <p class='static-page__text static-page__text--sinai'>
          The Sinai Palimpsests Project's original informational website remains available at <%= link_to 'sinaipalimpsests.org', 'http://sinaipalimpsests.org', target: '_blank', rel: 'noopener noreferrer' %>, with further background on the project's methods and discoveries. Its digitized images and manuscript descriptions have since been migrated to the Sinai Manuscripts Digital Library, joining the Monastery's other collections in a single, unified resource. Full credits for the project's scholars, staff, and imaging scientists appear below.
        </p>
      </div>

      <hr class='divider'>

      <div class='static-page__row'>
